"# This Environment
<!-- base-instructions-123; generated by Lazurio, do not edit: an edited file blocks every re-render of the Folder (a profile change, a refresh, a product upgrade) until it is restored; keep your own notes outside the generated files; {"preset":"hosted-organization-personal"} -->
## Identity, from the recorded handover
- Name: \`workspace\` (workspace-vm).
- Owner: Organization \`example\`.
- Assignment: assigned to user \`example\` (GitHub id 12345).
- Tailnet: Headscale node \`example-workspace\`.
- Host: virtualization-host \`example-host\`, a higher administration and recovery domain than this Environment.

## Preset \`hosted-organization-personal\` (version 1)
An Organization-owned work Remote Environment assigned to one user; agents act for them within their live rights. Personalspace is never present. Organization repositories live under \`organizations/<org>/\`; the user signs in with their own identity. Applications are owned by the OS service manager.

## Zones: what may reach what (decision 0246)
One user's Environments and clients form two zones. The **personal zone** holds the user's personal clients (laptop, phone) and their one personal Remote Environment; its Owner is the user. The **work zone** holds the work Remote Environments assigned to this user (possibly several, across Organizations of the same Conglomerate) and a work laptop issued by an Organization; its Owner is the Organization. The zone follows \`owner\` and \`operator\`, today bound to immutable GitHub IDs, never a Headscale user or a name; as the target a person is named by the issuer and subject of their Lazurio account (decision 0245).

- Inside a zone: personal client → personal Remote Environment; work laptop → work Remote Environment of the same Organization assigned to the same user. Each edge runs one way and none arises merely as the reverse of another (decision 0246). A route from an Environment to a device that the handover records is today's state: as the target only a Device of the Organization accepts SSH from an Environment, and nothing connects into a personal device (decision 0240, not yet deployed). Work Remote Environments have no route between each other but those of the section Routes into other Environments.
- From personal to work: yes. Personal clients and the personal Remote Environment reach every work Remote Environment of the same user (SSH and HTTPS).
- From work to personal: never. No work Remote Environment or work laptop has a grant to a personal Remote Environment or a personal laptop.
- A shared Team Remote Environment accepts only inbound connections from members of the live GitHub Team it is bound to (decisions 0147/0149) and never has an outbound grant.
- Nothing implicitly between users. A phone is not an SSH server.
- Everything holds inside one Conglomerate (one Headscale tailnet); a personal Remote Environment is a node of its home tailnet only, and no bridge is built between tailnets.
- Only its holder administers the Conglomerate Host and the servers under the Environments: Lazurio's operator where Lazurio runs the servers, and the live Organization Owner where the Organization runs them on its own infrastructure; only from devices approved for it, never by the Organization Admin role and never from an administrative station (decision 0245). An administrative station, an Organization Admin's Work Environment, leads only into the Environments of their Organization, and from the Admin's Personal Environment the way to them goes in two steps through that station (decision 0247).

**This Environment's zone.** This Environment is in the work zone of Organization \`example\`, assigned to one user. The user's personal clients and personal Remote Environment may reach it; it never reaches them. It reaches another Environment only by the routes of the section Routes into other Environments (decisions 0246 and 0247).

## Boundaries in this Environment
- Personalspace: never present in an Organization-owned Environment. Do not create or mount one and never copy personal data or sign-ins here.
- Organizations: repositories live under \`organizations/<org>/\`; each Organization is its own access boundary and its own git repository with its own \`AGENTS.md\`.
- Identity: the user's own sign-ins. GitHub is the authority for source code (repositories, review, Publication), and Headscale holds the network. Entry to Environments is to be held by the Lazurio account (the target of decision 0247); until that move is done, the existing path decides, a Team Environment's GitHub Team, say (\`manual/lazurio.md\`).
- Network: this Environment's network is one for every thread and session here, and Lazurio Machines manages its Tailscale. Never switch it, sign it out, point it at another login server or stop it: you would cut everyone else off. Reach another tailnet only the way the Environment offers, such as an approved headless client's socket (the section Adding a computer to a tailnet below) or that tailnet's own network space chosen for each command; when it offers none, tell the user (decision 0217, addendum 2026-10-31).

## Adding a computer to a tailnet
Establish the user, device and target tailnet, then choose its stable name. Add the computer to the user's existing account; a second computer creates neither another account nor another Environment. Run client commands on the source computer. If you run in a hosted Environment, prepare the procedure for the agent on the source computer; do not rename this Environment.
1. Verify the account identity and Headscale login server from signed-in Lazurio or the administrator of that tailnet. Do not derive the login from the OS account, email or target Environment. For a linked GitHub account use the lowercase login frozen when its personal Headscale user was created; without GitHub use its existing label. Do not link GitHub for a name. If the identity is unknown, find it or ask for it first.
2. Name a new computer \`<login>-<device>\`, for example \`example-user-macbook\` or \`example-user-windows\`. Use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, without emoji or defaults such as \`node\`, \`localhost\` or a random OS name. Choose the device label once and keep it across tailnets; distinguish two devices of the same kind with stable labels such as \`macbook-air\` and \`macbook-pro\`. Do not add an Organization or Conglomerate to the device name: the verified login server and DNS suffix distinguish the network. A name is neither identity nor permission. The \`-personal-vm\` suffix belongs to a hosted personal Environment, not a laptop.
3. Before enrollment inspect the existing client: \`tailscale debug prefs\` (\`ControlURL\`) and \`tailscale status --json\`. Do not switch, log out or reset a different active tailnet yourself. If an approved headless client exists for the target network, use its socket in every command (\`tailscale --socket=<socket> …\`). Do not create another client just for this task. A headless client of the same computer is named \`<login>-<device>-cli\`, at most one per computer and tailnet, with its own approval.
4. On first connection pass an explicit \`--hostname=<name>\` with the verified \`--login-server=<address>\` through the client's supported sign-in procedure. The user completes OIDC sign-in with their Lazurio account; if supported sign-in is unavailable, hand off to the administrator instead of improvising legacy enrollment. For a newly enrolled, still unapproved device with a wrong name, run \`tailscale set --hostname=<name>\` on the device; preserve its other settings. If the macOS CLI is outside PATH, use the client supplied by the installed app. Do not install a second daemon or change the OS computer name for this. Never manually run \`headscale nodes rename\` on the Conglomerate Host.
5. Verify the result: \`ControlURL\` matches the target network and \`Self.DNSName\` from \`tailscale status --json\` has exactly the chosen first label and the correct suffix. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback: node ID, machine key, final name, owner and IPv4/IPv6. A \`-1\` suffix or another name is not success: resolve a collision or an earlier manual rename with the administrator; do not delete another node. Do not rename an approved device just for convention: its name is part of exact access bindings, so a change needs a coordinated plan and new verification. Treat re-enrollment changing the node ID or machine key the same way.
6. Tailnet registration grants no access yet. For a work or Team Environment, the Organization Admin approves the exact device and its assigned path through the network administrator's managed procedure. Today a reviewed change by the Conglomerate Host owner admits a device to a personal Environment in a Conglomerate's tailnet; as the target, the Organization Admin approves it like any device (decision 0245). In Lazurio's personal tailnet, as the target, its user approves their own devices (decision 0246); until that is built, Lazurio's operator approves them. Each computer has its own binding and SSH key; never copy a private key. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback from step 5 for both computers and their exact network bindings; do not assume a device list in the Organization overview. Only after approval and deployment, if the target Environment offers SSH, continue in its Settings → SSH from your devices, pin its host key and verify \`ssh <alias> true\`. Distinguish registration, approval, deployment and the test; say what is missing.

## How the user works with you
The user sits at their own computer and works with you in the clients they prefer, possibly several at once: ChatGPT Desktop (formerly Codex Desktop) over SSH, T3 Code on the web or in its desktop app, and Lazurio MausBot. The handover does not record where the user connects from. Your harness tells you which client you run in; the user may be working in another one meanwhile. Your shell, files and processes are here, not on the user's computer.

- **Starting a preview.** Run work in progress from the worktree beside the stable application (\`manual/working-here.md\`, Stable applications and development previews). In the owning repository, verify the worktree path, the command and how it takes its host and port. Use a free port of its own bound to loopback; pass a Module its declared \`LAZURIO_RUNTIME_LISTENER_<ID>_*\`. Leave an unknown process on an occupied port alone and choose another port. Handle the preview's data and sign-ins under the owning repository's rules; do not guess a missing dependency.
- **The preview for the user.** This Environment has no Environment browser. Never write a bare \`localhost\` link into the chat: it would open on the user's computer, not here.
  - When you run in ChatGPT Desktop over SSH, open the preview in its built-in browser. Current versions open an SSH tunnel to the port themselves, on a random port of the user's computer. OpenAI does not document this and older versions cannot do it, so check that the page really loaded.
  - When you run in T3 Code or Lazurio MausBot, no port reaches the user. Tell them so, give the local address and branch and what remains unverified. Never redirect the stable address for the preview or expose the port through the gateway.
- **Application links.** A module's stable application from \`main\` has a hosted name through this Environment's gateway (decision 0146) that works in the user's normal browser on the Organization's network. Take it exactly from \`runtime.url\` in \`lazurio module status <Org>/<module> --json\` or from the Launchpad, never compose it from a naming convention; when \`runtime\` is \`null\`, the application has no hosted name, so say that instead of a link. \`lazurio chat link\` prints the link into this Environment's T3 Code.
- **Files from the user.** A file dragged into the ChatGPT Desktop chat arrives in \`~/.codex/attachments/\`; into T3 Code and Lazurio MausBot the user uploads it in the chat. Save what you will need later in \`~/Documents/<task>/\`.
- **Files for the user.** Save finished files in \`~/Documents/<task>/\`. The user has no direct access to this Environment's files and a \`/home/…\` path opens nothing on their computer, so hand over the result the way their client can: show an image in the chat, open a document in ChatGPT Desktop's preview. A large file (tens of MB) does not reliably pass through the preview; say so and offer a smaller or split version.
- **What you cannot reach.** You cannot reach the user's computer, its folders or the network drives mapped on it from here: a work Environment has no grant to clients. If you need a file from there, ask for it to be dragged into the chat; never ask for passwords or credentials.
- **Work only.** This Environment belongs to the Organization and serves work only. When you get a personal request, tell the user it does not belong here and point them to their personal environment; never store personal data here.
- **Chats.** Conversations from every client are stored here, in an Environment the Organization owns; they are work conversations. The user deletes and archives chats in their own client; do not delete session files yourself. In ChatGPT Desktop, a thread's menu (right-click the thread in the sidebar) offers Permanently delete only in Codex mode (the switcher at the top left); in ChatGPT mode it offers only Archive. When someone asks how to delete a thread, tell them.

## Enabled tools
The CLIs of the Lazurio catalog that agents in this Environment are to use: the required ones and those the user enabled in this Folder (\`lazurio tools list\`, \`lazurio tools enable|disable <tool>\`). Enabling is context, not authority or installation: it grants no access, installs nothing and pins no version. Report a missing or signed-out tool to the user and install or sign it in only with their consent; offer them a newer version (\`manual/troubleshooting.md\`).

- \`gh\` (required): GitHub CLI for work with repositories, pull requests, issues and reviews. Use \`gh\` for all GitHub work. Verify the signed-in identity with \`gh auth status\` before a connected operation; a sign-in is not proof of the right to an exact operation.
- \`executor\` (required): This Environment's MCP gateway for directly connected Integrations and custom MCP servers; it runs as a service on localhost only and Lazurio looks after it. Use the Integrations connected directly through the MCP server \`executor\` (its tools \`skills\` and \`execute\`) or with \`executor call tools <integration> <owner> <connection> <tool> '<json>'\`; \`executor tools integrations\` and \`executor tools search "<task>"\` show what is there. Check the service and the agents' connection with \`lazurio executor status\`; when it reports that Executor is not running or not finished, tell the user: on their instruction \`lazurio executor setup\` repairs it (Settings → Tools → executor offers the same). An externally visible write through an Integration (sending, publishing, deleting) is a Publication: prepare the work and leave it unfinished; the user's explicit “Publish” for it is the full mandate to finish it (\`manual/working-here.md\`).

### Integrations and MCP
The Integrations (connected apps) and custom MCP servers of this Environment are held by the Environment's Executor and by Composio with the user's account; \`lazurio integrations list --json\` prints what is connected and which way, and the rules are in \`manual/working-here.md\` (section Integrations). Integrations are never recorded in the Folder, and neither a connected app nor an available MCP server is consent to Publication.

## Connecting a computer and a ChatGPT Desktop project
The agent on the user's computer sets up the connection, not you. You are in the Environment the computer connects to, and the computer's private key does not belong here.
The handover gives no Launchpad address, so do not guess it. Ask whoever manages the Environment (in an Organization, the Organization Admin) to add it.
After a successful SSH test, the agent on the source computer ends its chat reply with a clickable Markdown link to ChatGPT Desktop taken exactly from the page. It does not add the project itself or bypass app security restrictions: the user clicks. Explain that if the first click adds no project, they can click the same link once again; this sometimes happens and is not a required double-click. Once successful, the user sees the Lazurio folder in the left sidebar. A single Organization project may simply be named after the Organization; distinguish several Environments of one Organization by kind as well, such as work and Team, and give a personal project its own personal label. Only the display name changes, never the SSH alias or remote path. If the link still fails, guide the user through adding the page's exact values manually in Settings → Connections → SSH.
A registered key gives no network access by itself. The network administrator decides which devices reach the network (Headscale and the SSH grant). Do not switch the Tailscale profile on the user's computer.

